LIFE for 16-bit OS/2 VIO/KBD
============================

This is a direct OS/2 character-mode port of the supplied NT-console LIFE.C.
It deliberately uses the classic 16-bit OS/2 APIs and assumes M_I386 is not
selected.

Platform substitutions
----------------------

  Win32 console / _kbhit / _getch     -> KbdCharIn(IO_NOWAIT)
  SetConsoleCursorPosition            -> VioSetCurPos
  console screen fill                 -> VioScrollUp with a blank cell
  cursor hide/show                    -> VioGetCurType / VioSetCurType
  WriteConsole / printf-for-display   -> VioWrtTTY / VioWrtCharStr
  Sleep                               -> DosSleep

The VIO and KBD handles are 0, the normal default handles for a fullscreen
16-bit OS/2 character application.

Important source repair
-----------------------

The supplied NT port contains:

    while (1==2) { /* ((ch=scr_csts()) || suspend) { */

inside create().  That disables the original editor/pause keyboard loop.
LIFEOS2.C restores the intended loop.  When initially editing a board, the
cursor is visible.  During normal generation it is hidden; pressing lowercase s
pauses and makes it visible again.  Uppercase 'S' cannot be a separate switch case
because ASCII 'S' (83) is also the BIOS/OS2 scan code used here for Delete; the
original source had that uppercase case commented out for the same reason.

Extended keys
-------------

KbdCharIn returns the ASCII byte and scan code together.  When chChar is 0 or
0xE0, LIFEOS2 returns chScan and marks the key as extended.  This preserves
the original BIOS-style scan codes used by LIFE:

    Up 72, Down 80, Left 75, Right 77, Ins 82, Del 83

Display
-------

The original DeSmet program used character codes 1 and 2 directly.  The NT
port mapped character 2 to the letter X.  OS/2 VIO can write the raw OEM
character, so this port restores those character codes.  LIFE C also uses
VioWrtCharStrAtt to cycle cell colours.

Building with a period Microsoft 16-bit OS/2 toolchain
-------------------------------------------------------

The supplied era reference material uses commands of this form:

    cl /c /F 2000 /I\path\to\os2h LIFEOS2.C
    link LIFEOS2,LIFE,,OS2,LIFEOS2

Exact compiler switches depend on the Microsoft C/OS/2 SDK version installed.
The important points are:

  * compile as 16-bit protected-mode OS/2, not M_I386/32-bit
  * make the supplied OS/2 include directory visible
  * link against OS2.LIB (or DOSCALLS.LIB where appropriate)
  * use LIFEOS2.DEF so the executable is NOTWINDOWCOMPAT + PROTMODE

The supplied OS2.H automatically chooses INCL_16 when M_I386 is absent;
LIFEOS2.C also explicitly selects INCL_16 before including OS2.H.

Files
-----

  LIFEOS2.C   - ported source
  LIFEOS2.DEF - 16-bit OS/2 module definition file
  README.OS2  - this file
